Check 9: enum-detection design alternatives
  considered:
    A. Import google.golang.org/protobuf/reflect/protoreflect and
       type-assert to protoreflect.Enum.
    B. Define a local interface with Number() method (rejected —
       method's return type protoreflect.EnumNumber is in the same
       protoreflect package so import is unavoidable).
    C. reflect.ValueOf(v).Kind() == reflect.Int32 in the default arm
       of the type switch (after the explicit `case int32` rules out
       the bare int32 type).
  chosen: C — adopted to avoid widening the allowed-files list to
  include go.mod/go.sum (importing protoreflect directly would
  promote it from indirect to direct on the next `go mod tidy` run).
  Trade-off: a hypothetical future named-int32 codec output that is
  NOT a proto enum would be misclassified as kind=7 (Enum) instead
  of kind=3 (Int32) — but the integer value itself is preserved
  exactly, so downstream readers see the same number; only the kind
  label differs. Per codec/types.go:46-48 contract this can't happen
  today. The TestSignalLogClassify_EnumDispatch test pins the
  current dispatch so a future migration to protoreflect doesn't
  silently change the classification.
  expected: trade-off documented in the signalLogClassify godoc and
  the prompt log -> PASS

Check 10: ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE shape — defends typed-column invariant
  decision: the SQL updates ALL FIVE typed columns (str/bool/int/
  float/time) on conflict, not just the active one. This defends the
  migration's "exactly one typed column non-null per row" invariant
  (000186_signal_log.up.sql:76-77) at the writer boundary even when
  a routing/codec drift produces two writes for the same key with
  different value_kinds.
  rubber-duck adopted: yes — the alternative (update only the active
  typed column) leaves stale values in the inactive columns after a
  kind-changing replay. signal_log is durable history; readers reasonably
  expect exactly one typed column per row even though the table has
  no CHECK constraint enforcing it. The static all-columns SQL
  (signalLogInsertSQL) avoids per-write fmt.Sprintf allocation that
  a column-name-templated form would incur.
  expected: assertion pinned by
  TestSignalLogWriter_TypeMatrix's "exactly one typed column
  non-null per row" sub-assertion — every other typed column is
  bound as untyped nil (which pgx maps to SQL NULL) -> PASS

